I’ve been on my career break for 8 months. During the first 3 months, I thoroughly explored Generative AI tools to identify potential use cases for my professional and personal life, ranging from chatbots and “vibe coding” to various agentic applications. I summarized my initial learning framework and resources in this article back in July.

To prepare for Data Science interviews, I adopted an “AI-first” mindset. I used Gen AI to “vibe code” projects like a SQL Quiz Generator incorporating business context and an LTV model for a subscription SaaS product. It was frustrating that despite performing well in most interviews, I didn’t receive any job offers. Following the rejections, I came up with an “innovative” self-promotion idea: a paper titled “Productize Myself in Six Months (no-ads)” to showcase my qualifications to potential employers. This also proved unsuccessful. Consequently, after launching my Pu’er Tea business in China in October, I returned to conventional applications and interviews.

I was interviewing with several small and medium-sized companies through cold applications or recruiter outreach. While these companies might not represent the entire industry, two of them actively encouraged the use of AI assistance during the technical interview. This was a huge relief for me, as I hadn’t coded regularly for a while and now primarily rely on “vibe coding” for simple analytics tasks. This acceptance of AI tools could signal an emerging trend for DS interviews. The ability to simply write queries is becoming less of a unique strength to evaluate a candidate; instead, the ability to define problems from ambiguity and select the appropriate analytical approach is becoming more critical.

I was quite disappointed that none of these interviews resulted in official job offers. So, I turned to the traditional method: a referral. My most recent interview opportunity is with my previous company, Intuit, referred by a friend there. The role focuses on causal inference and advanced analytics to improve decision quality. Although I’m usually reluctant to spend time solely preparing for interviews, I am treating this as an opportunity to deepen my econometrics/statistics knowledge and sharpen my AI application skills for this specific purpose.

Shifting to an “Agent-First” Mindset
The AI landscape has evolved rapidly in the second half of 2025, with new AI feature launches and breakthroughs in infrastructure and foundation models occurring almost every week. Google Antigravity, a newly launched agentic development platform, caught my attention recently, though I haven’t yet found a perfect use case for myself. With the daily influx of different types of agents on social media, my mindset has shifted from “AI-first” to “agent-first”: I wanted to build a causal inference agent, even though I had no idea what it might look like initially.

To get started, I prompted in the Gemini chatbot:

“I want to build a causal inference AI agent to automate causal inference tasks to evaluate metrics impact, using a B2B SaaS product as an example. Can you write a project plan and build a prototype tool using Python?”

The suggested project plan made sense, especially for building something scalable end-to-end in a business environment. It clearly outlined steps, objectives, key tasks, and deliverables along with timelines.

