Additionally, Gemini provided prototype scripts in Python covering the core elements of causal inference analysis — such as model specification, identification, estimation, and robustness checks — all leveraging a simulated dataset.

The prototype script was a great starting point, as I wanted to build a quick proof-of-concept for this side project. I followed up with the prompt:

“Can you please create a frontend UI that wraps up each step in the prototype, with options to select methods and parameters?”

Gemini recommended Streamlit, a tool I was hearing about for the first time, to build a Python-based interactive web application. To further streamline the implementation, I asked if I could use Google Antigravity to do the job, and received a “Yes” with step-by-step instructions. Hence, I switched to Antigravity to develop and deploy the causal inference application. Since I was new to this vibe coding IDE, I had no idea where to start. However, the beauty of vibe coding is that I can simply ask the Agent Manager to complete tasks for me, such as creating a new workspace for the project— the very first step in app development.

The initial version provided by Antigravity, based on my high-level product requirements, already exceeded my expectations. I would estimate a workload of two weeks given my minimal experience in app development without AI assistance; now, it took me less than an hour to get something up and running.

Refining the Tool: The Causal Inference Application
To make the tool truly useful, I needed to apply my expertise in theoretical econometrics and empirical data analysis to make each step straightforward while validating the accuracy of the results. Through many conversational iterations, I finally launched the MVP on Streamlit! It consists of three main tabs:

User Guide: Step-by-step instructions to apply the tool.
Exploratory Analysis: Data preprocessing, data summary, and visualization functions.
Causal Analysis: Configurations for estimation method and model specifications, and detailed model output.
The application is intentionally designed to not be fully autonomous, as causal inference requires domain expertise in econometrics and statistics. Therefore, it is always recommended to use the tool with at least basic knowledge of causal inference to avoid misusing the methods and misinterpreting the results. In the future, I may explore ways to enhance it to be more autonomous, given that the ultimate goal is to make it a true AI agent.

Case Study Walkthrough
Now, I’m going to demonstrate the application using a simulated dataset of 1,000 account-level rows with variables such as account_value, conversion, feature_adoption, and customer_segment.

Exploratory Data Analysis

A quick data summary can be viewed on the “Exploratory Analysis” tab. The tool also builds in common data preprocessing procedures such as missing imputation, outlier treatment, log/standard transformations, bucketing, and customized filters. My simulated dataset is fairly clean, so I don’t need to apply these additional processing steps for simplicity.

