Identification & Model Specification

We need to identify all possible confounders when specifying the causal model. Essentially, we want to identify variables that correlate with both feature adoption (treatment) and account value (outcome), which can often be done through a correlation matrix. However, it is vital to apply economic intuition when specifying a causal relationship inferred from correlations.

From the correlation matrix and bar charts, we can see that besides feature_adoption, customer_segment and historical_usage are also highly correlated with account_value. These two variables align with economic intuition and will be considered key confounders in the causal estimation.

